## Vim Delete Commands

In Normal Mode, the `d` command is used to delete text.
It can be combined with motions and numbers.

| Action | Result |
|--------|--------|
| `dd` | Delete current line |
| `3dd` | Delete the next three lines |
| `dw` | Delete the current word |
| `d3w` | Delete the next three words |
| `d4h` | Delete four characters to the left |

---

## How It Works

The structure is:

[number] + command + motion

Examples:

- `dd` → delete line
- `3dd` → delete 3 lines
- `dw` → delete word
- `d3w` → delete 3 words

You can combine `d` with any motion command.

----------------------------------

## Vim Change Commands

In Normal Mode, the `c` command is used to change text.
It deletes the specified text and switches to Insert Mode.

| Action | Result |
|--------|--------|
| `cc` | Change current line |
| `cw` | Change current word |
| `c3w` | Change the next three words |
| `c5h` | Change five characters to the left |

---

## How It Works

Structure:

[number] + operator + motion

- `c` → change operator
- `w`, `h`, `$`, etc. → motion
- number → how many times

Examples:

- `cc` → delete entire line and enter Insert Mode  
- `cw` → delete word and enter Insert Mode  
- `c3w` → delete 3 words and enter Insert Mode  

After using `c`, Vim automatically switches to Insert Mode.
Press `Esc` to return to Normal Mode.

--------------------------------------

## Vim Yank (Copy) Commands

In Normal Mode, the `y` command is used to copy (yank) text.

| Action | Result |
|--------|--------|
| `yy` | Yank current line |
| `3yy` | Yank the next three lines |
| `yw` | Yank the current word |
| `y$` | Yank to the end of the line |

---

## How It Works

Structure:

[number] + operator + motion

- `y` → yank (copy)
- `w`, `$`, `h`, etc. → motion
- number → how many times

Examples:

- `yy` → copy one line  
- `3yy` → copy 3 lines  
- `yw` → copy one word  
- `y$` → copy from cursor to end of line  

To paste:
- `p` → paste below / after cursor  
- `P` → paste above / before cursor

------------------------------------------------

## Vim Paste Commands

In Normal Mode, `p` and `P` are used to paste (put) previously yanked or deleted text.

| Action | Result |
|--------|--------|
| `p` | Put (paste) after cursor |
| `P` | Put (paste) before cursor |

---

## How It Works

- `p` → paste after the cursor  
- `P` → paste before the cursor  

If you copied or deleted a **line**, it pastes as a new line.
If you copied a **word or characters**, it pastes inline at the cursor position.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

## Vim Insert Mode Commands

These commands are used in Normal Mode to enter Insert Mode.

| Input | Purpose |
|-------|----------|
| `a` | Enter insert mode right after the cursor |
| `A` | Enter insert mode at the end of the line |
| `i` | Enter insert mode right before the cursor |
| `I` | Enter insert mode at the beginning of the line |
| `o` | Enter insert mode on a blank line after the cursor |
| `O` | Enter insert mode on a blank line before the cursor |

---

## Explanation

- `i` → insert before cursor  
- `a` → append (after cursor)  
- `I` → beginning of line  
- `A` → end of line  
- `o` → open new line below  
- `O` → open new line above  

Press `Esc` to return to Normal Mode.

----------------------------------------------------------------

## Vim Command Mode Commands

These commands are used in Command Mode (Last Line Mode).
Press `:` to enter Command Mode.

| Input | Purpose |
|-------|----------|
| `:w` | Write the current file to the filesystem |
| `:w filename` | Save a copy of the current file as filename |
| `:w!` | Force writing to the current file |
| `:1` | Go to line number 1 (or any given number) |
| `:e filename` | Open filename |
| `:q` | Quit if no changes made to file |
| `:q!` | Quit without saving changes |

---

## How It Works

- `:` → enters Command Mode  
- `:w` → save  
- `:q` → quit  
- `:wq` → save and quit  
- `:q!` → force quit without saving  

Press `Enter` after typing the command to execute it.

